[background arg="res://sprites/24062026133858-0001 (1).jpg" fade="0.0"]
join sushi left
audio music "res://Soundtracks/Sushi_song.mp3" [bus="" loop="true" sync=""]
sushi: S\: …Evening, Chef.
character: C\: Hi? You’re… Sushi.
sushi: S\: That I am. How are you this fine evening?
- Ok
	character: …I’m okay.
	sushi: That’s good to hear. I’m of adequate status myself as well.
	character: Why are you speaking like that?
	sushi: I feel using more complex words and phrases helps people understand my intentions better than colloquial language. Is there an issue with it? Do you not understand my verbalising?
	character: …Only a little bit. Have you ever seen the movie V for Vengeance? “Verily, this vichyssoise of verbiage veers most verbose…”
	character: [wave amp=5 freq=10] Ring any bells?
	sushi: …No, I have not. That sentence strikes an interest in me, however. Is this movie made for connoisseurs of the language?
	character: Only a little bit. This guy in a Guy Fawkes mask, V, is a freedom-fighter and tries to blow up Parliament in a dystopian, fascist society. Great film. I’m pretty sure it’s on Superior Video, if you’re that interested.
	sushi: …Hm. It interests me quite a bit. Does it create focus and attention on the growing capitalistic society we live in, where the wealth gap continues to grow larger and more severe and thus fosters a world where the poor stay poor and the rich stay rich?
	character: It’s more of an action movie than anything else, but I’d say so. If nothing else, it’s a good film for the explosions.
	sushi: You said you had access to it on your cellular phone? May I use it to watch said entertainment?
	character: Sure, sure. I’ll drag some of the spare chairs over so we can both watch, just pull it up.
	jump sushiMOVIE/
- Confused?
	character: Confused, really.
	sushi: Is it because of my constitution as an edible eastern cuisine?
	character: Yeah, partially. And your insistence on using complicated words, like a sesquipedalian.
	sushi: …Sesquipedalian? I didn’t take you for an English aficionado like myself.
	character: [wave amp=50 freq=2] …Yeah. Definitely.
	character: I know what aficionado means.
	sushi: …I can hear the hesitation in your voice. Aficionado is a synonym for the word connoisseur.
	character: …Thank you. So, do you like big words, or is that just a trait of yours?
	sushi: Many people have said that my diction is rather grandiloquent, but I feel that the usage of such a dialect creates a more understanding and honest relationship between whoever I’m conversing with and I.
	character: ‘Grandiloquent’, she says, knowing full well I don’t know what that word means.
	sushi: Grandiloquent, someone who uses complex wording and vocabulary for purposes of boasting and extollation. You may just ask without the use of sarcasm, you realise?
	character: Yeah, I get that, but… It feels humiliating to ask questions about language to someone who was born about 2 minutes ago, when I’ve been speaking it my whole life.
	sushi: Don’t feel humiliated by your inadequacy. I’m sure compared to you, my skills regarding culinary practices are lacklustre.
	character: …True.
	sushi: I actually had a question regarding that, if you may hear it.
	character: Shoot.
	sushi: Would you be able to teach me how to prepare a meal? As you may have picked up, I consider myself to be an intellectual, and you seem to be well-versed in that field, given your occupation.
	character: I… Sure, I guess.
	jump sushiCOOKING/
- Not great
	character: Not great, all things considered.
	sushi: May I indulge in my curiosity and ask why?
	character: My… partner, they broke up with me. It’s fine, nothing to concern yourself with. My turn for a question now; why the vocabulary?
	sushi: [wave amp=50 freq=2] ...Oh.
	sushi: Well, I apologise for bringing the subject into light. As for the question about my lexicon, I feel that words with more complexity bring a sort of regality and professionalism into the day-to-day, which helps foster a more honest relationship by increasing understanding of my intentions.
	character: …I didn’t catch any of that. Not a lick.
	sushi: …By increasing the complexity of my dialect, my general presence is more professional and thus aids in creating a more honest and easy-to-understand relationship with the people I am around.
	character: Still nothin’.
	sushi: [wave amp=50 freq=2] Good grief; were you raised by neanderthals?
	sushi: I apologise… Essentially, this way of speaking makes a more understandable conversation.
	character: Right…
	sushi: You look disheartened. I apologise again for my outburst.
	character: It’s all cool. Also, I severely doubt that speaking like that is any more understandable than like this.
	sushi: Why would it not be?
	character: It’s just not. Sure, technically it’s able to convey your emotions clearer, but who really knows what the word ‘grandiloquent’ means?
	sushi: The vocabulary of nowadays is rather lacking. Is it not your issue that you don’t understand my vocabulary?
	character: …Did you just ‘not my problem’ me?
	sushi: I believe I did. It’s really the fault of the American Education System, however.
	character: I can’t argue that.
	sushi: Indeed.
	character: ...
	sushi: …And once more, you look disheartened.
	character: Yeah. I’m just coming to terms with how bad my education was.
	sushi: Well, while I may not be a qualified educator, surely I’d be able to assist you in revision.
	character: I’d… I’d actually like that. Thanks.
	jump sushiTEACHING/
	set {SushiMood} = "Mood"